  {
   "id": "opening-cliff",
   "category": "content",
   "text": "The show loses most starters almost immediately: on episode 1, 67.1 of every 100 were watching near the start but only 12 remained at the 2% mark. Every episode shows the same early cliff before viewership rebounds later.",
   "recommendation": "This week, open with the single strongest demo or clip in the first minute and push all housekeeping and setup talk until after it. Compare next episode's early hold against this week's to see if the cliff shrinks."
  },
  {
   "id": "trim-loose-banter",
   "category": "content",
   "text": "Episode 1's biggest single exit came about 7 minutes in, when 7.9 of every 100 viewers left during loose setup talk (\"we're going to try some stuff... pull back the curtain\"). We can't say the words caused it, but the timing lines up with unstructured chat.",
   "recommendation": "Test a tighter run of show: write one-line intros for each segment, cap freeform banter between segments, and move a concrete showcase into that early slot next episode. Watch whether the early exit spike softens."
  },
  {
   "id": "subscribe-ask-collapsed",
   "category": "audience",
   "text": "Episode 1 converted 8.8 subscribers per 1,000 views (13.2 on DesignerTom, 5.6 on Dive Club). Episode 3 converted 0 on both channels. Episode 4 got 5.7 on Dive Club but 0 on DesignerTom. Something episode 1 did stopped happening, especially on DesignerTom.",
   "recommendation": "Rewatch episode 1 and note when and how you asked people to subscribe, then script that ask back into this week's show on both channels. Add a pinned comment and description line with the subscribe ask on every DesignerTom upload."
  },
  {
   "id": "discovery-surfaces-idle",
   "category": "distribution",
   "text": "64.3 of every 100 YouTube views come from existing subscribers. Discovery surfaces are barely working: search brings 4.3, related videos 7, playlists 0.6, and end screens 0.1 of the share. The show is mostly preaching to people it already has.",
   "recommendation": "This week, add an end screen to every past episode pointing to the next one, build a single series playlist and link it in every description, and rewrite titles and descriptions around search terms designers actually type."
  },
  {
   "id": "x-audience-untapped",
   "category": "promotion",
   "text": "Half the audience watches on X: 50.4 of all watching, 15,282 plays versus 15,043 YouTube views. But subscribers, end screens, and playlists only accrue on YouTube, so half the audience never enters the funnel that grows the show.",
   "recommendation": "During this week's X simulcast, pin the YouTube link in the first reply, say the YouTube handle out loud twice, and offer one YouTube-only reason to click over, like a bonus segment or chat shoutouts. Track whether YouTube live viewers rise."
  },
  {
   "id": "live-audience-slide",
   "category": "promotion",
   "text": "Peak live viewers fell from 90 on episode 1 to 53 on episode 3, and chat fell from 383 messages to 169. The latest show recovered to 72 peak viewers but only 92 chat messages, so fewer people are talking even when they show up.",
   "recommendation": "Post a day-before announcement with the topic and guest on both channels and on X, schedule the live event so notifications fire, and run one named chat prompt every segment, like a poll or question, to pull lurkers into the conversation."
  }
 ]
